  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-2\\cdot {e}^{4x-3}=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "We can use the laws of exponents and the algebra of limits to rewrite the given limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-2\\cdot {e}^{4x-3} & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{4x-3} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{-3}\\cdot {e}^{4x}) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {e}^{-3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{4x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{e}^{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{4})^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={({e}^{4})}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/assets/6590b80777c49b3d018fa38ce79c7fb14103365f783dac18135b8d3637ee724d.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{4})^{x}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nThe negative factor $-\\frac{2}{{e}^{3}}$ changes the sign of the limit. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{2}{{e}^{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{4})^{x}=-\\frac{2}{{e}^{3}}\\cdot \\infty =-\\infty .\n$$",
